{"step_index":24,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T11:21:00Z","content":"--- **1 OSTEOARTHRITIS** **DEFINITION** Osteoarthritis OA is a chronic progressive degenerative disorder of synovial joints characterized by loss of articular cartilage subchondral bone changes osteophyte formation and varying degrees of synovial inflammation resulting in pain stiffness and functional limitation **CAUSES / RISK FACTORS** **Primary OA** Occurs without a single identifiable cause Risk factors • Increasing age • Female sex • Obesity • Genetic predisposition • Previous joint injury • Repetitive joint loading **Secondary OA** Occurs due to an identifiable condition • Previous fracture • Joint infection • Rheumatoid arthritis • Congenital joint abnormality • Ligament injury • Meniscal injury • Avascular necrosis • Metabolic disorders **COMMON SITES** • Knee • Hip • Spine • Hands • First MTP joint • Shoulder and other joints less commonly **PATHOPHYSIOLOGY** Mechanical/biological stress ↓ Cartilage matrix degeneration ↓ Loss of proteoglycans and collagen integrity ↓ Cartilage thinning and fissuring ↓ Subchondral sclerosis ↓ Osteophyte formation ↓ Joint space narrowing ↓ Pain + stiffness + deformity **CLINICAL FEATURES** **Symptoms** • Joint pain usually worse with activity • Pain relieved by rest initially • Morning stiffness usually less than 30 minutes • Stiffness after prolonged inactivity • Difficulty walking • Difficulty climbing stairs • Reduced functional activity **Signs** • Joint tenderness • Crepitus • Reduced ROM • Bony enlargement • Joint line tenderness • Muscle weakness • Deformity in advanced disease **Hand OA** • Heberdens nodes DIP joints • Bouchards nodes PIP joints **DEFORMITIES** **Knee** • Varus deformity • Valgus deformity **Hip** • Flexion deformity • External rotation • Adduction deformity **Hands** • Heberdens and Bouchards nodes **RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES** X ray shows • Non uniform joint space narrowing • Osteophytes • Subchondral sclerosis • Subchondral cysts • Bony remodeling • Deformity in advanced disease Classic mnemonic LOSS • Loss of joint space • Osteophytes • Subchondral sclerosis • Subchondral cysts **COMPLICATIONS** • Severe pain • Joint deformity • Contractures • Muscle wasting • Reduced mobility • Disability • Secondary instability • Reduced quality of life **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** **Medical** • Patient education • Weight reduction • Activity modification • Paracetamol/analgesics where appropriate • NSAIDs when appropriate • Topical NSAIDs • Intra articular corticosteroid injection in selected cases • Management of comorbidities **Physiotherapy** • ROM exercises • Quadriceps strengthening • Hip strengthening • Aerobic exercise • Aquatic exercise • Gait training • Balance exercises • Assistive devices • Bracing when indicated **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** Indicated in severe symptomatic disease with significant functional limitation despite conservative treatment **Procedures** • Arthroscopic procedures in selected circumstances • Osteotomy in selected younger patients with appropriate deformity • Total knee replacement • Total hip replacement • Other joint replacement procedures --- **2 RHEUMATOID ARTHRITIS** **DEFINITION** Rheumatoid arthritis RA is a chronic systemic autoimmune inflammatory disease characterized primarily by persistent inflammatory synovitis of multiple joints leading to cartilage destruction bone erosion deformity and extra articular manifestations **CAUSES / RISK FACTORS** Exact cause is unknown Important factors • Genetic predisposition • Autoimmune mechanisms • Female sex • Smoking • Environmental factors • Hormonal factors • Certain infections may act as triggers **PATHOPHYSIOLOGY** Autoimmune activation ↓ Synovial inflammation ↓ Pannus formation ↓ Cartilage destruction ↓ Marginal bone erosions ↓ Ligament/tendon damage ↓ Joint instability and deformity **CLINICAL FEATURES** **General** • Fatigue • Malaise • Fever • Loss of appetite • Weight loss **Joint** • Symmetrical polyarthritis • Pain • Swelling • Warmth • Prolonged morning stiffness typically 30 60 minutes • Reduced ROM Commonly affects • MCP • PIP • Wrist • MTP • Knee • Elbow • Shoulder • DIP joints are usually spared **CHARACTERISTIC DEFORMITIES** **Hand** • Ulnar deviation • Swan neck deformity • Boutonniere deformity • Z thumb deformity • Wrist deformity **Foot** • Hallux valgus • MTP subluxation • Claw toes **Cervical spine** • Atlantoaxial instability/subluxation **COMPLICATIONS** **Joint** • Destruction • Subluxation • Ankylosis • Deformity • Disability **Extra articular** • Rheumatoid nodules • Vasculitis • Interstitial lung disease • Pleural disease • Pericarditis • Eye involvement • Anemia • Osteoporosis • Increased cardiovascular risk **RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES** Early • Soft tissue swelling • Periarticular osteopenia Later • Uniform joint space narrowing • Marginal erosions • Subluxation • Deformity • Ankylosis in advanced disease Cervical spine • Atlantoaxial instability • Erosive changes **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** **Medical** Early treatment with DMARDs is central May include • Methotrexate • Hydroxychloroquine • Sulfasalazine • Leflunomide • Biological/targeted DMARDs • NSAIDs • Corticosteroids for selected indications **Physiotherapy** • Pain relief • ROM exercises • Muscle strengthening • Joint protection • Splinting • Energy conservation • Hydrotherapy • Functional training • Assistive devices **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** Indications • Severe joint destruction • Persistent pain • Functional disability • Deformity Procedures • Synovectomy • Tendon repair • Arthrodesis • Joint replacement • Cervical stabilization when required --- **3 ANKYLOSING SPONDYLITIS** **DEFINITION** Ankylosing spondylitis AS is a chronic inflammatory rheumatic disease predominantly affecting the axial skeleton and sacroiliac joints causing inflammatory back pain progressive spinal stiffness and in advanced disease spinal fusion It belongs to the axial spondyloarthritis spectrum **CAUSES / RISK FACTORS** • Strong genetic association with HLA B27 • Family history • Immune mediated mechanisms • Environmental factors **CLINICAL FEATURES** **Early** • Insidious low back pain • Buttock pain • Sacroiliac pain • Morning stiffness • Improvement with exercise • Worsening with rest • Night pain **Progressive** • Reduced spinal mobility • Chest expansion • Hip involvement • Fatigue **DEFORMITIES** Advanced disease may produce • Loss of lumbar lordosis • Increased thoracic kyphosis • Forward head posture • Hip flexion deformity • Fixed spinal posture Classically described as a bamboo spine in advanced radiographic disease **COMPLICATIONS** • Severe spinal stiffness • Hip arthritis • Osteoporosis • Vertebral fractures • Spinal cord injury after fracture • Uveitis • Cardiovascular involvement • Restrictive chest wall mechanics • Functional disability **RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES** **X ray** • Sacroiliitis • Vertebral squaring • Vertebral corner erosions • Syndesmophytes • Bridging of vertebrae • Bamboo spine • Hip joint changes **MRI** Can demonstrate • Active sacroiliitis • Bone marrow edema • Early inflammatory changes **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** **Medical** • NSAIDs • Biologic agents such as TNF inhibitors or IL 17 inhibitors when indicated • Other targeted therapy in selected patients **Physiotherapy** Very important • Spinal mobility exercises • Postural correction • Chest expansion exercises • Breathing exercises • Strengthening • Hydrotherapy • Stretching • Aerobic conditioning • Patient education • Maintain upright posture • Avoid prolonged flexed posture • Regular exercise **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** Indications • Severe hip destruction • Fixed severe deformity • Neurological compression • Unstable spinal fracture Procedures • Total hip replacement • Corrective spinal osteotomy in selected severe deformities • Spinal stabilization/decompression when indicated --- **4 GOUTY ARTHRITIS** **DEFINITION** Gout is an inflammatory arthritis caused by deposition of monosodium urate crystals in joints and tissues as a result of persistent hyperuricemia **CAUSES / RISK FACTORS** **Primary** Usually related to impaired uric acid excretion or increased production **Secondary** • Chronic kidney disease • Myeloproliferative disorders • Certain medications • Tumour lysis • High cell turnover Risk factors • Obesity • Alcohol excess • High purine diet • Metabolic syndrome • Renal disease • Family history **COMMON SITES** • First MTP joint podagra • Ankle • Knee • Midfoot • Elbow • Wrist • Fingers **CLINICAL FEATURES** **Acute attack** • Sudden severe pain • Redness • Swelling • Warmth • Extreme tenderness • Often occurs at night **Chronic gout** • Recurrent attacks • Tophi • Joint destruction • Chronic stiffness **Tophi** Deposits may occur in • Ear • Fingers • Toes • Elbow • Achilles tendon **COMPLICATIONS** • Chronic arthritis • Tophi • Joint destruction • Kidney stones • Chronic kidney disease • Reduced function **DEFORMITIES** • Irregular joint enlargement • Destructive deformity • Tophi related deformity **RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES** **X ray** • Well defined punched out erosions • Overhanging edges • Preserved joint space until later disease • Soft tissue tophi • Bone destruction in advanced disease **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** **Acute attack** • NSAIDs • Colchicine • Corticosteroids • Rest and ice • Avoid joint trauma **Long term** • Weight management • Reduce alcohol • Dietary modification • Adequate hydration • Urate lowering therapy ◦ Allopurinol ◦ Febuxostat in appropriate patients • Treatment should be individualized based on renal function and comorbidities **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** Usually not required May be indicated for • Large symptomatic tophi • Nerve compression • Severe joint destruction • Recurrent infection over tophi • Severe functional impairment Procedures • Tophus excision • Joint reconstruction/replacement in selected cases --- **5 PSORIATIC ARTHRITIS** **DEFINITION** Psoriatic arthritis PsA is a chronic inflammatory musculoskeletal disease associated with psoriasis involving peripheral joints the axial skeleton entheses and digits **CAUSES / RISK FACTORS** • Genetic predisposition • Psoriasis • Immune dysregulation • Family history • Environmental factors **CLINICAL FEATURES** **Joint** • Asymmetrical arthritis • Pain • Swelling • Stiffness • Morning stiffness **Characteristic** • Dactylitis sausage digit • Enthesitis • Commonly ◦ Achilles tendon ◦ Plantar fascia **Skin** • Psoriatic plaques • Nail pitting • Nail dystrophy **Spine** • Inflammatory back pain in axial disease **DEFORMITIES** • Joint deformity • Flexion deformities • Pencil in cup deformity in severe disease • Arthritis mutilans • Ankylosis **COMPLICATIONS** • Permanent joint damage • Severe functional disability • Spinal stiffness • Eye inflammation • Cardiovascular/metabolic comorbidity **RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES** • Erosions • New bone formation • Asymmetric joint space loss • Periostitis • Pencil in cup deformity • Ankylosis • Sacroiliitis • Syndesmophytes **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** **Medical** • NSAIDs • DMARDs ◦ Methotrexate ◦ Sulfasalazine in selected peripheral disease • Biologic agents • Targeted synthetic DMARDs • Treatment of psoriasis **Physiotherapy** • ROM exercises • Strengthening • Hydrotherapy • Postural exercises • Joint protection • Functional training **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** For advanced joint destruction • Synovectomy in selected cases • Arthrodesis • Joint replacement • Tendon procedures --- **6 HAEMOPHILIC ARTHRITIS** **DEFINITION** Haemophilic arthritis is a chronic destructive arthropathy caused by recurrent bleeding into joints hemarthrosis in patients with haemophilia It commonly affects • Knee • Ankle • Elbow **CAUSE** Usually occurs in • Hemophilia A factor VIII deficiency • Hemophilia B factor IX deficiency Repeated hemarthroses produce chronic synovial inflammation and cartilage destruction **PATHOPHYSIOLOGY** Factor deficiency ↓ Recurrent hemarthrosis ↓ Blood accumulation in joint ↓ Synovial hypertrophy ↓ Iron deposition ↓ Cartilage destruction ↓ Joint space narrowing ↓ Deformity **CLINICAL FEATURES** **Acute hemarthrosis** • Sudden joint pain • Swelling • Warmth • Restricted movement • Protective muscle spasm **Chronic** • Recurrent swelling • Chronic pain • Stiffness • Muscle wasting • Flexion contractures • Reduced ROM **DEFORMITIES** • Knee flexion deformity • Elbow flexion deformity • Ankle equinus • Joint enlargement • Muscle wasting **COMPLICATIONS** • Chronic arthropathy • Contractures • Joint deformity • Muscle wasting • Secondary osteoarthritis • Severe disability • Recurrent bleeding **RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES** Early • Soft tissue swelling • Joint effusion Advanced • Epiphyseal enlargement • Subchondral cysts • Joint space narrowing • Osteophytes • Erosions • Irregular articular surfaces MRI can identify • Synovial hypertrophy • Hemosiderin deposition • Cartilage damage **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** **Acute bleed** Factor replacement is the main treatment Additional • Rest • Ice • Compression where appropriate • Elevation • Analgesia • Protected movement initially **Long term** • Factor prophylaxis where indicated **Physiotherapy** • Muscle strengthening • ROM exercises • Joint protection • Weight management **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** For advanced arthropathy • Synovectomy • Joint replacement • Arthroscopic procedures in selected cases • Corrective procedures for severe deformity Adequate factor replacement is essential around surgery --- **7 STILLS DISEASE / JUVENILE IDIOPATHIC ARTHRITIS** **DEFINITION** Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis Stills disease is a subtype of juvenile idiopathic arthritis characterized by arthritis with systemic inflammation fever and often an evanescent salmon pink rash **CAUSES** Exact cause is unknown Possible mechanisms • Autoinflammatory immune dysfunction • Genetic predisposition • Environmental triggers **CLINICAL FEATURES** **Systemic** • High spiking fever • Evanescent salmon pink rash • Fatigue • Malaise • Lymphadenopathy • Hepatosplenomegaly • Serositis **Joint** • Pain • Swelling • Stiffness • Reduced ROM • Polyarthritis **DEFORMITIES** Untreated chronic disease may produce • Flexion deformities • Growth disturbance • Limb length discrepancy • Joint destruction • Contractures **COMPLICATIONS** • Growth retardation • Joint destruction • Contractures • Osteoporosis • Macrophage activation syndrome • Chronic disability • Amyloidosis in longstanding inflammatory disease **RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES** Early • Soft tissue swelling • Osteopenia Later • Joint space narrowing • Growth abnormalities • Erosions • Deformity **CONSERVATIVE / MEDICAL MANAGEMENT** • NSAIDs • Corticosteroids • Methotrexate for persistent arthritis • Biologic agents especially IL 1 or IL 6 targeted therapy where indicated • Physiotherapy • Occupational therapy • Nutritional support **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** Rarely required in active systemic disease In severe chronic joint destruction • Synovectomy in selected cases • Joint replacement • Corrective surgery --- **8 CHARCOT JOINT** **DEFINITION** Charcot joint neuropathic arthropathy is a progressive destructive arthropathy resulting from loss of protective sensation and proprioception leading to repetitive microtrauma joint instability deformity and destruction **CAUSES** Most common • Diabetes mellitus with peripheral neuropathy Other causes • Tabes dorsalis • Syringomyelia • Peripheral neuropathy • Spinal cord disease • Congenital sensory neuropathy **COMMON SITES** • Foot and ankle • Midfoot • Knee • Hip • Spine **PATHOPHYSIOLOGY** Loss of protective sensation ↓ Repeated unrecognized trauma ↓ Inflammation + bone resorption ↓ Joint instability ↓ Fragmentation and dislocation ↓ Severe deformity **CLINICAL FEATURES** • Swelling • Warmth • Redness • Instability • Deformity • Reduced pain despite severe structural damage • Crepitus • Altered gait **Foot** • Characteristic rocker bottom deformity may occur **COMPLICATIONS** • Severe deformity • Ulceration • Infection • Osteomyelitis • Amputation • Fractures • Instability • Loss of mobility **RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES** Early • Soft tissue swelling • Subchondral changes Advanced • Joint destruction • Fragmentation • Subluxation • Dislocation • Bone sclerosis • Osteophytes • Debris • Deformity A classic description is bag of bones appearance in advanced disease **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** **Acute stage** • Complete/off loading as indicated • Total contact casting or appropriate immobilization • Custom orthoses • Regular skin inspection • Glycemic control • Foot care education **Chronic** • Custom footwear • Orthoses • Protected weight bearing • Gait training • Fall prevention **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** Indications • Severe deformity • Instability • Recurrent ulceration • Failure of conservative treatment • Severe structural destruction Procedures • Arthrodesis • Corrective osteotomy • Exostectomy in selected cases • Reconstruction • Amputation for uncontrollable infection or non reconstructable limb **CONNECTIVE TISSUE DISORDERS** --- **9 SYSTEMIC LUPUS ERYTHEMATOSUS SLE** **DEFINITION** Systemic lupus erythematosus SLE is a chronic systemic autoimmune disease characterized by autoantibody production and immune mediated inflammation affecting multiple organs including the skin joints kidneys nervous system blood and cardiovascular system **CAUSES / RISK FACTORS** • Genetic predisposition • Female sex • Hormonal factors • Environmental triggers • Ultraviolet light • Certain medications • Infections may act as triggers **CLINICAL FEATURES** **General** • Fatigue • Fever • Weight loss **Musculoskeletal** • Symmetrical joint pain • Non erosive inflammatory arthritis • Morning stiffness • Myalgia **Skin** • Malar rash • Photosensitivity • Discoid lesions **Other systems** • Oral ulcers • Hair loss • Renal disease • Neurological manifestations • Serositis • Hematological abnormalities **DEFORMITIES** Usually non erosive arthritis but chronic disease may cause • Jaccoud arthropathy • Ulnar deviation • Swan neck like deformities • Tendon abnormalities These may be partially reducible unlike fixed erosive RA deformities **COMPLICATIONS** • Lupus nephritis • Cardiovascular disease • Stroke • Seizures • Anemia • Thrombocytopenia • Osteoporosis • Avascular necrosis particularly with prolonged corticosteroid exposure • Infection **RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES** Usually • Soft tissue swelling • Osteopenia may occur Unlike RA • Marked erosions are usually absent X rays may show • Jaccoud type deformities without typical erosive destruction **CONSERVATIVE / MEDICAL MANAGEMENT** • Patient education • Sun protection • Exercise • Hydroxychloroquine in most appropriate patients • NSAIDs for selected musculoskeletal symptoms • Corticosteroids for active disease when indicated • Immunosuppressive therapy • Biologic therapy in selected patients • Treatment of individual organ involvement **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** Not usually directed at SLE itself May be required for • Severe joint destruction • Avascular necrosis • Fracture • Tendon deformity Procedures • Joint replacement • Corrective surgery • Fracture fixation --- **10 SCLERODERMA / SYSTEMIC SCLEROSIS** **DEFINITION** Systemic sclerosis scleroderma is a chronic autoimmune connective tissue disease characterized by vascular abnormalities immune dysregulation and excessive collagen deposition resulting in fibrosis and hardening of skin and potentially internal organs **TYPES** 1 Limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis Skin involvement mainly distal to elbows/knees and face 2 Diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis More extensive skin involvement including trunk and proximal limbs **CAUSES / RISK FACTORS** Exact cause unknown Factors • Genetic predisposition • Autoimmune mechanisms • Environmental exposures • Vascular dysfunction **CLINICAL FEATURES** **Skin** • Skin thickening • Tight shiny skin • Loss of skin folds • Reduced mobility **Hands** • Raynaud phenomenon • Puffy fingers initially • Sclerodactyly • Digital ulcers • Contractures **Face** • Tight facial skin • Reduced mouth opening • Mask like appearance **Systemic** • Dysphagia • Reflux • Interstitial lung disease • Pulmonary hypertension • Renal crisis **DEFORMITIES** • Flexion contractures of fingers • Sclerodactyly • Reduced mouth opening • Hand deformity • Joint stiffness **COMPLICATIONS** • Digital ulcers • Gangrene • Pulmonary fibrosis • Pulmonary hypertension • Renal crisis • Cardiac disease • Esophageal dysfunction • Severe functional disability **RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES** May show • Soft tissue calcification • Acro osteolysis • Joint space changes • Contractures Chest imaging may demonstrate • Interstitial lung disease/fibrosis **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** **Medical** • Vasodilator therapy for Raynaud phenomenon • Immunosuppressive therapy for selected systemic disease • Proton pump inhibitors for reflux • Treatment of pulmonary hypertension • Renal blood pressure management **Physiotherapy** • ROM exercises • Stretching • Hand exercises • Splinting when indicated • Breathing exercises • Aerobic conditioning • Functional training • Skin protection and education **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** May include • Debridement of severe digital ulcers • Management of gangrene • Joint reconstruction in selected cases • Surgical treatment of severe contractures --- **11 DERMATOMYOSITIS** **DEFINITION** Dermatomyositis is an autoimmune inflammatory disease characterized by proximal skeletal muscle weakness and characteristic cutaneous manifestations **CAUSES / RISK FACTORS** Exact cause unknown Possible associations • Autoimmune predisposition • Genetic factors • Environmental triggers • In some adults association with malignancy **CLINICAL FEATURES** **Muscle** • Symmetrical proximal muscle weakness • Difficulty climbing stairs • Difficulty rising from a chair • Difficulty lifting arms • Difficulty combing hair • Neck flexor weakness **Skin** • Heliotrope rash • Gottron papules • Photosensitive rash • Shawl sign • V sign **Other** • Dysphagia • Respiratory muscle involvement • Fatigue **DEFORMITIES / FUNCTIONAL PROBLEMS** • Flexion contractures • Poor posture • Muscle wasting • Reduced mobility • Difficulty with transfers • Gait abnormalities • Children may develop calcinosis **COMPLICATIONS** • Severe muscle weakness • Dysphagia • Aspiration • Respiratory failure • Interstitial lung disease • Calcinosis • Malignancy association in some adults • Disability **RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES** There are no characteristic plain X ray findings MRI muscle may show • Muscle edema • Inflammatory changes • Muscle atrophy in chronic disease Other investigations • Elevated CK • EMG abnormalities • Muscle biopsy in selected cases **CONSERVATIVE / MEDICAL MANAGEMENT** **Medical** • Corticosteroids • Immunosuppressive agents • IVIG in selected cases • Other immunomodulatory/biologic therapies as appropriate • Sun protection • Treatment of dysphagia and respiratory complications **Physiotherapy** • Gentle ROM • Gradual strengthening after disease control • Aerobic conditioning • Functional training • Energy conservation • Respiratory exercises where indicated • Avoid aggressive exercise during uncontrolled active myositis **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** Usually not required for the primary disease May be required for • Severe calcinosis • Contractures • Secondary complications --- **12 POLIOMYELITIS** **Important** Poliomyelitis is primarily an infectious viral disease of the nervous system not a connective tissue disease It is included here because it is part of the requested syllabus **DEFINITION** Poliomyelitis is an acute viral infection caused by poliovirus primarily affecting the anterior horn cells of the spinal cord and sometimes the brainstem resulting in flaccid muscle weakness or paralysis **CAUSE** Causative organism • Poliovirus Transmission • Mainly fecal oral route • Occasionally oral oral transmission **PATHOPHYSIOLOGY** Poliovirus infection ↓ Viral replication ↓ CNS invasion in severe disease ↓ Anterior horn cell destruction ↓ Lower motor neuron dysfunction ↓ Flaccid paralysis **CLINICAL FEATURES** **Prodromal** • Fever • Headache • Malaise • Sore throat • Vomiting **Paralytic disease** • Acute asymmetric weakness • Flaccid paralysis • Reduced muscle tone • Reduced/absent reflexes • Muscle wasting • Fasciculations may occur • Sensory function usually preserved **Respiratory involvement** • Intercostal weakness • Diaphragmatic weakness • Respiratory failure **DEFORMITIES** Long term sequelae may include • Equinus deformity • Knee flexion deformity • Hip deformity • Scoliosis • Limb shortening • Foot deformity • Joint contractures **COMPLICATIONS** • Respiratory failure • Aspiration • Permanent paralysis • Muscle wasting • Contractures • Skeletal deformity • Post polio syndrome years later • Reduced functional independence **RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES** X rays are not diagnostic of acute poliomyelitis Chronic cases may show • Muscle wasting related osteopenia • Limb shortening • Deformities • Scoliosis • Joint contractures **CONSERVATIVE MANAGEMENT** **Acute** • Supportive medical care • Respiratory support • Pain control • Prevention of contractures • Appropriate positioning **Rehabilitation** • ROM exercises • Muscle strengthening of functioning muscles • Orthoses • Gait training • Energy conservation • Assistive devices • Respiratory physiotherapy where indicated **Post polio syndrome** • Pacing • Energy conservation • Avoid overuse weakness • Appropriate low to moderate exercise • Functional adaptation **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** For residual deformities • Tendon transfer • Corrective osteotomy • Arthrodesis • Limb length procedures in selected cases • Spinal surgery for severe scoliosis • Orthopaedic reconstruction --- **13 MIXED CONNECTIVE TISSUE DISEASE MCTD** **DEFINITION** Mixed connective tissue disease MCTD is an autoimmune connective tissue disorder characterized by overlapping clinical features of systemic lupus erythematosus systemic sclerosis polymyositis/dermatomyositis and rheumatoid arthritis usually associated with high levels of anti U1 RNP antibodies **CAUSES** Exact cause is unknown Associated with • Genetic predisposition • Autoimmune dysregulation • Environmental triggers **CLINICAL FEATURES** **Musculoskeletal** • Polyarthritis • Arthralgia • Myalgia • Proximal muscle weakness **Vascular** • Raynaud phenomenon **Skin** • Puffy fingers • Sclerodactyly like changes • Rash **Other** • Dysphagia • Esophageal dysfunction • Interstitial lung disease • Pulmonary hypertension • Fatigue **DEFORMITIES** May develop • Joint deformity • Contractures • Sclerodactyly • Muscle wasting • Reduced hand function **COMPLICATIONS** • Pulmonary hypertension • Interstitial lung disease • Esophageal dysfunction • Cardiac involvement • Renal involvement usually less prominent than in classic SLE • Severe muscle weakness • Functional disability **RADIOLOGICAL FEATURES** No single pathognomonic radiographic appearance May demonstrate • Joint space narrowing • Soft tissue swelling • Erosive arthritis in some patients • Interstitial lung disease on chest X ray/HRCT • Muscle inflammation/edema on MRI **INVESTIGATIONS** **Blood** • ANA • Anti U1 RNP • CBC • ESR/CRP • CK • Renal and liver function **Other** • Pulmonary function tests • Echocardiography • HRCT chest where indicated • MRI muscle if myositis is suspected **CONSERVATIVE / MEDICAL MANAGEMENT** Treatment depends on the dominant manifestations May include • NSAIDs • Corticosteroids • Hydroxychloroquine • Methotrexate • Other immunosuppressive drugs • Vasodilators for Raynaud phenomenon • Treatment for pulmonary hypertension • Treatment of interstitial lung disease **Physiotherapy** • ROM exercises • Muscle strengthening • Aerobic conditioning • Breathing exercises • Hand therapy • Joint protection • Energy conservation • Functional training **SURGICAL MANAGEMENT** Usually not required for the primary disease May be considered for • Severe joint destruction • Fixed d